WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.240
- This all started when Dua Lipa got sued

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.480
for posting a photo of herself.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.670
Earlier this year, British
singer Dua Lipa was sued

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.600
after allegedly posting a paparazzi photo

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:09.920
of herself on Instagram.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.260
The agency which owns the picture

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.920
alleged that the Instagram
post violated their copyright

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.140
in the image.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.500
The singer's fans were
understandably confused.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.840
Why couldn't she post a
mundane image of herself

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.650
standing in line at an airport?

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.380
Well, according to Integral Images,

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.960
which owns the copyright,
Lipa's Instagram feed

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.980
acts as a marketing tool for her music.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.570
The agency alleges that the photographs

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.950
intentionally infringed
on Integral's copyright

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.950
and undercut its ability
to profit from the photos.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.710
After all, Dua Lipa didn't
pay to post that photo.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.510
The lawsuit seeks these statutory damages

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.080
for an intentional copyright violation

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.420
that amounts to $150,000.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.130
The agency is also asking
for an order stopping Lipa

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.610
from further acts of infringement.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.620
Now, Dua Lipa was not
the only famous person

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.610
sued for posting a picture of herself.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.570
Splash News sued Jennifer Lopez

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.340
for posting a Splash photo of her

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.050
holding hands with Alex Rodriguez in 2017.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.350
A photographer sued Jessica Simpson

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:01.640
after she posted a photo of herself

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.560
leaving the Bowery Hotel in New York.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.850
The photographer said that he
had only licensed the photo

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.600
for limited use to the "Daily Mail,"

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.930
but Simpson and her social
media managers tweeted it out

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.010
without licensing it.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.960
Splash News and Picture Agency
sued Liam Hemsworth in 2018,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.860
claiming he posted its
photos of him on Instagram

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.100
without the company's permission.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.520
Paparazzo Robert Barbera
sued Ariana Grande

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.790
after she posted two of
his photos on her Instagram

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.520
in August of 2018.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.170
And photographer Christopher
Pasatieri sued 50 Cent

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.720
after 50 posted photographs
taken by Pasatieri

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.120
during a 2014 reunion concert with G-Unit.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.640
So do paparazzi
photographers have the right

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.460
to stalk famous people, profit
from selling their images,

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.390
then turn around and sue the famous person

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.555
for posting photographs of themselves?

00:01:41.555 --> 00:01:44.460
(dramatic music)

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:45.293
Hey, Legal Eagles,

00:01:45.293 --> 00:01:47.220
it's time to think like a copyright lawyer

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.520
because some of this is
not exactly intuitive.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.080
Now, we've talked about copyright

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.310
many times on this channel.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.650
Copyright is a legal device

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.060
that gives the creator of a work of art

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.540
the right to control
how their work is used.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.170
When we use the word creator or author,

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.280
that could mean writers,
musicians, photographers,

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.560
architects, software developers,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.380
or even people who design
boats for a living.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.790
All of them are considered authors

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.440
under the American copyright law,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.260
and the things that they
produce are works of art.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.590
The protection for works of art
comes from the Constitution.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.870
Article I, Section 8, Clause
8 gives Congress the power

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.184
to, quote, "promote the progress
of science and useful arts,

00:02:17.184 --> 00:02:20.170
by securing for limited times
to authors and inventors

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.450
the exclusive right to
their respective writings

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:23.340
and discoveries."

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.462
Scientific pursuits like machines

00:02:24.462 --> 00:02:26.901
or useful items are
generally given patents,

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:28.990
but artistic creations like novels

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.790
and photographs are
covered by copyright law.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.130
And because copyright is
provided for in the Constitution,

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.160
it's exclusively bound by federal law.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.040
And under 17 USC 106, if you
have a copyright in something,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.120
you have the exclusive right
to do a number of things,

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.120
including make copies of that work of art,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.610
make adaptations and derivatives,

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.150
distribute the work,
publicly perform a play

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.410
or perform it in front of others,

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.660
display your work in public,

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.500
digitally transmit sound recordings,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.950
and in some circumstances, the
right to receive attribution

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.040
and prevent a destruction of a work.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.080
And these are the rights
that photographers have

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.230
when it comes to their photographs.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.530
According to the US Copyright Office,

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.220
the owner of a work is
generally the person

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:05.310
who takes the photo.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.670
Generally, the only
time this isn't the case

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.420
is when there is a work-for-hire contract

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.860
or you have an
employee/employer situation.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.830
And generally, you don't own the copyright

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.090
just because you are
the subject of a photo.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:15.980
This could even be the case

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.130
when a person hires a
photographer to take photos

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.720
like a wedding photographer.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.850
It depends on what's in the contract.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:23.310
But if that contract is silent,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.840
then the photographer owns the copyright

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.050
to those photos of the wedding

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.630
unless the photographer agreed in writing

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.980
to transfer or license the
copyrights to that someone else,

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.630
presumably the bride and groom.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.680
And the damages for a single
instance of infringement

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:35.610
can be quite steep.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.610
Under the Copyright Act, a
copyright owner is entitled

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.810
to an award of actual
damages and disgorgement

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.940
of all the infringer's profits
attributable to infringement.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.430
And in the alternative, the
plaintiff can elect an award

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.510
of statutory damages for
an amount up to $150,000

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:49.540
for each infringement.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.140
And that takes us to the
paparazzi versus celebrities.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.270
In the internet age,

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.180
it's dramatically changed what's at stake

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:56.570
for paparazzi photographers.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.250
In the old days, they
could take photographs

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.470
and sell them to the highest bidder,

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.810
and this was usually a
magazine or a newspaper.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:03.570
Now, with the internet and social media,

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.530
there's a whole new
market for digital images.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.249
This is a blessing and a curse.

00:04:07.249 --> 00:04:09.160
Photographers can get their work seen

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:11.110
all over the world almost instantly,

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.670
but it also undercuts the
market for exclusive images

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.320
and made it much easier to infringe.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.080
Also, since everyone has
a camera in their pocket,

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:20.970
there's no longer exclusivity

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.320
when it comes to
photographs of celebrities.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.980
And meanwhile, celebrities themselves

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:26.320
are now content creators.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.580
They make a lot of money by
posting images of themselves

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:29.790
on social media accounts.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.990
And the top influencer accounts,
like Cristiano Ronaldo,

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.950
can get up to a million dollars per post.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:36.750
And as you can see, The
Rock, Ariana Grande,

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.320
several sisters Kardashian,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.460
and several family members Jenner

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.590
are also believed to earn
hundreds of thousands

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:43.940
of dollars per post.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.100
Photographers and their
agencies claim it isn't fair

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.300
for celebrities to distribute
images of themselves

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.937
without paying for a license

00:04:49.937 --> 00:04:52.110
for the person who took that photograph.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.500
This is the dynamic that has
created the current environment

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.700
where celeb photographers are suing

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.050
the same people they photograph.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.760
Now, there are lots of these cases,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.500
but let's talk about one of the biggest,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.220
Xposure versus Kardashian.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.840
The UK photo agency licensed
pictures of Kardashian

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.020
leaving a Miami restaurant
to the "Daily Mail."

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.360
But when Kardashian used
those very same images

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.294
on her Instagram feed
without their permission,

00:05:12.294 --> 00:05:14.580
it was available to all 67 million people

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.570
who follow Khloe Kardashian.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.400
Xposure sued Kardashian for
copyright infringement in 2017

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.620
seeking damages of more
than $175,000 for the post.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.780
Not that it would have been a defense,

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.140
but the Instagram post
didn't even credit Xposure.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.700
And to add insult to injury,

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.810
Kardashian or her team altered the image

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.730
to remove the copyright
management information,

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.220
the watermark that showed Xposure

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:35.220
as the copyright owner of the image.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.160
The copyright management
information, or CMI,

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.420
refers to the watermark indicating
where the image is from.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.316
You might've seen things like Via Getty,

00:05:42.316 --> 00:05:44.330
which does not mean it was taken

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:45.560
by a person named Via Getty.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.280
It means that Getty owns the rights

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.490
to that particular image.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:50.510
Now, we don't know what
the final terms were

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:51.480
in this particular case,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.000
Kardashian and Xposure
settled the case out of court

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.700
and Kardashian deleted the post.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:56.960
Kardashian apparently acknowledged

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.780
that she had indeed committed
copyright infringement

00:05:58.780 --> 00:05:59.950
with her Instagram posts.

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:01.180
She tweeted, quote, "Yes!

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.770
I have to license some
of the images first.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:04.110
A paparazzi sued me in the past

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:05.900
for reposting images of myself.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.500
So now it just takes me a little longer

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.290
because I have to go
and license the images

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.567
so they don't get my," money bag emoji.

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:12.670
"Makes no sense."

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:14.160
Yeah, the law doesn't make sense to Khloe.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.030
She, after all, is not the
Kardashian that became a lawyer.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.640
And in fairness, other celebrities agree.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.520
They contend that they are
being stalked every day

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.620
by people who want to make
money from their image

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:24.520
and likeness.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.440
And to be fair, paparazzi photogs

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.130
sometimes use special lenses

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.060
to take photos of people
inside their homes.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.610
Famous people like Princess Diana

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.040
had been literally hounded to death

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.500
by overzealous photographers.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:35.370
But then on the other hand,

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:36.670
there are people like Lisa Rinna,

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.190
who just says to hell
with it and intentionally

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:41.950
and repeatedly posts images
that she hasn't licensed

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.850
and blatantly violating the Copyright Act.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:46.110
Rinna has now been sued
for over a million dollars

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.328
for posting eight photographs
that she didn't license.

00:06:48.328 --> 00:06:50.430
Rinna said that if the
photographer had demanded

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.970
a lower amount, a nominal
fee, she would have paid.

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:54.740
Rinna and her lawyers claimed
that the copyright law

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.330
was never intended to allow photographers

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:59.437
to profit from images of
celebrities and, quote,

00:06:59.437 --> 00:07:01.890
"then turn around and sue
those same celebrities

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.420
for posting their own
images on social media."

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.140
Well, I hate to be that
guy, but actually, Lisa,

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.810
this is exactly what the
Copyright Act was designed to do.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:11.870
The act gives creators
the right to sue anyone

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:12.930
who infringes on their work,

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.468
even if that person
happens to be the subject

00:07:14.468 --> 00:07:16.200
of the photograph.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.120
But not every celebrity has struck out

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.870
making arguments to the contrary.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:22.850
Now, in 2018, Gigi Hadid
was sued by a photographer

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.710
who took a photograph of her
leaving her apartment building.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.420
Hadid even struck a pose for him.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:30.430
The photographer worked for
the agency Xclusive-Lee.

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:32.700
Hadid posted the image to her Instagram

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:34.350
without permission or payment.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.240
The agency filed an infringement lawsuit

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.610
seeking statutory damages.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.790
The law seemed to be
clear cut in this case.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:43.240
Hadid posted an image of
herself that she didn't own.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.500
This should be a slam dunk
for the agency, right?

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:47.640
Well, not exactly because Hadid's lawyer

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.960
got the case dismissed on a technicality.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.890
The agency failed to
register the photographs

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:53.430
before filing the lawsuit.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:55.940
Now, you don't have to
file with the Copyright Act

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:57.430
to get a copyright in something,

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.800
but filing your copyright
with the Copyright Office

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.560
gives you a bunch of benefits,

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.030
and the Supreme Court has now ruled

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:04.860
that actual registration of a copyright

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.170
is a prerequisite to
filing a copyright action,

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:09.070
which resolved a long running split

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.290
in the US circuit courts.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:11.130
The New York District Court

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.410
held that the Supreme Court's decision

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:14.490
foreclosed the paparazzi agency's

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.870
copyright infringement
claim against Hadid.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.040
However, this lawsuit
turned out to be notable

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.570
for other reasons because Hadid
raised some novel theories

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.730
that other celebrities
are now trying to develop

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.421
to defend themselves
against paparazzi lawsuits.

00:08:26.421 --> 00:08:29.190
Hadid's first theory was
that she co-authored the work

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:30.960
since she posed and smiled.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.600
Under this theory, she's entitled
to ownership of the image,

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.200
quote, "because she posed for the camera

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:37.330
and thus contributed many of the elements

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.320
that the copyright law seeks to protect."

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:40.250
Now, this is a stretch,

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:42.570
but it's not entirely without legal basis.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:45.020
A joint work is a work prepared
by two or more individuals

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:46.880
with the intention that
their separate contributions

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.670
be merged into a single work.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:51.810
Co-authors own the work's
copyright jointly and equally

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.750
unless the authors make
an agreement otherwise.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.217
Each joint author has
the right to exercise

00:08:55.217 --> 00:08:57.100
any and all of the
exclusive rights inherent

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:57.933
in the joint work.

00:08:57.933 --> 00:08:59.440
Now the primary problem with this argument

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.820
is that for a work to be a
product of two or more creators,

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:03.330
there must be a shared intent.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:05.280
The photographer certainly
didn't agree with Hadid

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.970
that this was a collaboration.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:07.930
The picture was unplanned

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:11.190
and happened when Hadid
spontaneously left the apartment.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:13.410
But really, the whole
point of paparazzi photos

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:15.990
is that they're taken without
the celebrity's permission,

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:18.730
and that was argued in
this particular case,

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.540
so it's hard to argue the
mutually exclusive arguments

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.510
that this was both unplanned and unwanted,

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:27.730
but also it was a collaboration
where everyone intended

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.160
for their efforts to become a joint work.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.690
Hadid also argued that
her use of the photographs

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:33.890
should be considered a fair use,

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:35.870
which we've talked a lot
about on this channel.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.570
This is definitely not your
standard fair use argument.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:40.870
And Hadid argued that when
she reposted the images,

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:43.420
she did it with a different
purpose than the agency.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:44.480
Her use was personal,

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.640
whereas the agency wanted
to exploit her popularity

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.040
for commercial purposes.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:50.440
She also argued that, without
her contribution to the image,

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:51.880
it wouldn't really exist.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:53.590
Now, the court didn't
address this argument

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:55.620
because the case was decided
on procedural grounds,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.350
but Hadid sort of misses the mark

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.000
when she makes this argument.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.290
The purpose of her use
really wasn't that different

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:02.650
from the paparazzi's use.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:07.560
It was to exploit her
popularity in a candid photo.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:08.510
She wasn't, for example,

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:10.620
criticizing the composition of the photo

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.420
or using the photo for
some educational purpose.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:15.700
This is why people license photos

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.040
that are taken by other people.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.470
She was effectively using the photo

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.960
for exactly the same reason
the paparazzi took the photo

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:22.793
in the first place.

00:10:22.793 --> 00:10:25.495
So while Hadid made some novel arguments,

00:10:25.495 --> 00:10:28.310
they probably wouldn't hold
water and she's probably

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:31.050
not gonna change copyright laws overnight.

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.220
Now what about the right of publicity?

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:35.320
Some celebrities have
tried to sue paparazzi

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:36.560
on the theory that the photographers

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.130
who profit off of someone's images

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.100
are actually violating the
celebrities right of publicity.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.670
Wide receiver Odell Beckham,
Jr. sued Splash News

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:46.227
after paparazzi took
photos of an injured OBJ

00:10:46.227 --> 00:10:48.220
outside his home in New Jersey.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.390
The photographer sold
the image to Splash News

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:51.730
and Picture Agency.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:53.457
Beckham posted the image to Instagram

00:10:53.457 --> 00:10:54.760
and Splash News sued him.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.160
Beckham filed a counterclaim,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.980
arguing that this was
an attempt to extort him

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:00.100
in violation of his right to publicity.

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:02.570
After all, you have a right to benefit

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:04.460
from your own name and likeness,

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:07.420
otherwise, everyone could
simply use photographs of you

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.040
in a commercial context,

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:10.560
so there's gotta be a
line somewhere, right?

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.307
And Beckham argued in
the complaint, quote,

00:11:12.307 --> 00:11:13.860
"Splash's licensing of photographs

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.370
of an injured Beckham not
only exploit's Beckham's image

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.580
for personal financial gain,
but is also patently gruesome."

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.500
And while this generally
relies on state law,

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:23.360
the right to publicity
generally protects a person

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:25.340
from having their likeness
used for advertising

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:27.651
or other commercial purposes
without their permission.

00:11:27.651 --> 00:11:30.240
Of course, this runs straight
into the First Amendment

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:33.080
because you have a
right to make the speech

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:34.280
that you want to make.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:38.070
And of course, in the case
of Odell Beckham Jr's injury,

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.100
that's certainly a newsworthy event,

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.410
which makes it receive even
more First Amendment protection.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.120
So generally, the right of publicity

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.190
means that a photographer has
the right to take photographs

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:50.070
of famous people and sell or
license those photographs,

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.500
but they can't sell products
with someone's image on them.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:54.510
So if you to happen to
get a great photograph

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.720
of Odell Beckham, Jr., you
could license that photograph

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.620
to a newspaper or magazine or sell it,

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:02.560
but you could not, however, sell T-shirts

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.270
or mugs bearing the face
of Odell Beckham, Jr.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:09.700
Only he can do that or someone
that has his permission.

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:11.070
Now, the OBJ case settled

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:12.430
before the court addressed his arguments,

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.650
but generally, they were
probably going to lose

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:16.820
since the right of a
photographer to take the picture

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:19.360
of a newsworthy person is
pretty well established.

